%T A401415 2,4,1,3,2,6,2,5,1,3,2,8,2,6,4,1,4,2,6,2,6,4,1,4,2,6,2,6,2,7,1,4,2,4,
%U A401415 2,6,2,6,1,3,3,8,2,4,3,7,4,2,5,2,4,2,5
%N A401415 Variation of A400185: the same rules apply except that poles can only see other poles that are located directly east, west, north or south of their square.
%C A401415 The taller poles are concentrated along diagonal lines of the square spiral, see the attached images, with the majority of these being on the four diagonals leading away from the central square; these contain the final square before the next square that has neighboring poles in only one direction. This is in contrast to A400185 and A401467 where the pole heights increase fairly uniformly as one moves away from the central square.
%C A401415 In the first 250000 terms the tallest pole is a(228483) = 218.
%H A401415 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A401415/b401415.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A401415 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A401415/a401415_1.png">Image of the first 1088 terms on the square spiral</a>. The colors are graduated across the spectrum, from red to violet, to show their relative size.
%H A401415 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A401415/a401415.png">Image of the first 100000 terms on the square spiral</a>.
%e A401415 The spiral begins:
%e A401415                                 .
%e A401415                                 .
%e A401415     1---4---2---5---2---4---2   4
%e A401415     |                       |   |
%e A401415     3   1---4---3---4---1   3   6
%e A401415     |   |               |   |   |
%e A401415     2   5   1---2---1   3   7   2
%e A401415     |   |   |       |   |   |   |
%e A401415     6   2   3   0---1   4   2   6
%e A401415     |   |   |           |   |   |
%e A401415     2   5   1---3---3---1   5   2
%e A401415     |   |                   |   |
%e A401415     5   1---4---2---6---3---2   4
%e A401415     |                           |
%e A401415     1---3---2---8---2---6---4---1
%e A401415 .
%e A401415 .
%e A401415 a(8) = 3 as the pole at that position can see one pole to its east, a(7) = 3, and two poles to its north, a(1) = 1 and a(2) = 1. If all directions were considered, as in A400185, then eight poles would be visible.
